  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "The boiling points of $\\mathrm{HF}, \\mathrm{HCl}, \\mathrm{HBr}$ and $\\mathrm{HI}$ follow the order",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "sPj81",
      "text": "HF $>\\mathrm{HCl}>\\mathrm{HBr}>\\mathrm{Hl}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "pRzcy",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{HF}>\\mathrm{Hl}>\\mathrm{HBr}>\\mathrm{HCl}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "vDNlM",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{Hl}>\\mathrm{HBr}>\\mathrm{HCl}>\\mathrm{H}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "eZazf",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{HCl}>\\mathrm{HF}>\\mathrm{HBr}>\\mathrm{Hl}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Due to hydrogen bonding, HF shows highest boiling point.\r\nThereafter van der Waals' force decide the boiling point.\r\nLarger the size or molecules mass, greater is the van der Waals' forces. Hence, higher is the boiling point.\r\n$\\because$ van der Waals' forces is more for HI than HBr, which is in turn is more than HCl.\r\nHence, correct order is $\\mathrm{HF}>\\mathrm{Hl}>\\mathrm{HBr}>\\mathrm{HCl}$",
    "image": null
  },
